{"text": "SUMMARY OF SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH:\nENVIRONMENTAL TOBACCO SMOKE\nAND AIRLINE CABIN AIR QUALITY\n\nMargaret E. Mattson, et. National Institutes of Health\n(Rockville, Md.), \u201cPassive Smoking on Commercial Airline\nFlights,\u201d Journal of the American Medical Association, Vol. 261,\nNo. 6, February 10, 1989.\n\nBackground: Assessed in-flight nicotine exposure by using\npersonal nicotine monitors and measuring levels of urinary\ncotinine. Involved nine subjects, including four flight\nattendants and five passengers, on four flights. The same\nsubjects participated in all flights. (Eight of the subjects\nwere employees of Air Canada, which has banned smoking on most\nflights.)\n\nAttendants were assigned to either smoking or nonsmoking\nsections. The five passengers were seated in and around the\nsmoking sections. Passengers were not sampled in nonsmoking\nareas farther from the border \u201cbecause of the expectation that\nexposures there would be very low.\" Each subject wore a passive\nmonitor to collect nicotine particles. Subjects provided urine\nsamples and completed questionnaires before and after each flight.\n\nAll subjects were nonsmokers who volunteered for the study and\nwere aware of its general objectives. All had signed a consent\nform which stated that ETS causes health problems. Subjects\ncompleted post-flight questionnaires that suggested symptoms and\nsuggested a linkage of these symptoms with ETS. The study made\nno attempt to address other sources of air quality problems, such\nas humidity levels or ozone.\n\nFindings: Minimal levels of nicotine were found in subjects\nseated in the nonsmoking border section. These levels were\nwithin ranges reported in previous studies. ETS exposure among\nflight attendants was minimal in both the smoking and nonsmoking\nsections; exposure for flight attendants was comparable to that\nof the passengers.\n\nSeveral of the reported symptoms, such as dry mouth, scratchy\nthroat, sneezing and headache, \u201cwere not significantly related to\nnicotine exposure.\u201d\n\nVentilation was cited as \"an important factor in the levels of\nair nicotine.\" The two planes with 100 percent fresh air had\nsignificantly lower levels than those with 50 percent recycled\nair. (The study notes that recycled air systems are becoming\nmore common in newer aircraft as fuel economy measures.)\n\nSspesttzoz",
